<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shroud of Turin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Holy Shroud</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Shroud of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> is a linen cloth bearing the faint image of a man, venerated by many as the burial cloth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. It is first documented at Lirey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the 1350s, was acquired by the house of Savoy in 1453 and moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a> to Turin in 1578. Radiocarbon tests in 1988 dated the linen to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and the former king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umberto-ii">Umberto II</a> bequeathed it to the Pope on his death in 1983.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shroud-of-turin.jpg" alt="Shroud of Turin" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dianelos Georgoudis · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turin_shroud_positive_and_negative_displaying_original_color_information_708_x_465_pixels_94_KB.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
